{"date": "2026-11-01", "post_id": 1507027, "title": "Colors of the Moon", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-1-colors-of-the-moon/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> What color is the Moon? It depends on the night -- and the time. Outside of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-atmosphere/earths-atmosphere-a-multi-layered-cake/\">Earth's atmosphere</a>, the <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/moon-albedo\">dark Moon</a>, which shines by reflected sunlight, appears a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2003-november-9-apollo-17-lunarscape-a-magnificent-desolation/\">magnificent</a>ly brown-tinged gray. The <a href=\"https://greenflash.photo/portfolio/colors-of-the-moon/\">featured image</a> highlights a collection of apparent colors of the full moon on Earth, documented by one astrophotographer over 10 years from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/G0EUqGhYZ6Y\">different locations</a> across <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>. A <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-november-11-blood-moon-ice-giant/\">red</a>- or <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">yellow</a>-colored moon usually indicates a moon seen <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-1-buck-moon-and-belt-of-venus/\">near the horizon</a>. There, some of the blue light has been scattered away by a long path through the <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-is-earths-atmosphere/\">Earth's atmosphere</a>, sometimes laden with fine dust. A <a href=\"https://www.loc.gov/everyday-mysteries/categories/astronomy/item/what-is-a-blue-moon-is-it-ever-really-blue/\">blue-colored moon</a> is more rare and can indicate an atmosphere carrying larger <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/a-dusty-journey-146122/\">dust particles</a>. What created the purple moon is <a href=\"https://cdn.akc.org/content/hero/bulldog_rib_bone_hero.jpg\">unclear</a> -- it may be a combination of several effects. The last image captures the total <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-7-two-eclipses-of-saros-133/\">lunar eclipse</a> of 2018 July -- where the moon, in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-25-earths-shadow-visualized-with-lunar-eclipses/\">Earth's shadow</a>, appeared a faint red -- due to light <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Refraction\">refracted</a> through air around the Earth. <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261101\">Have you</a> ever seen an unusually colorful Moon?<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong><a href=\"https://apod.com/fap/ap261102.html\">open space</a>", "credit": "<a href=\"https://greenflash.photo/about-me/\">Marcella Giulia Pace</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://greenflash.photo/about-me/\">Marcella Giulia Pace</a>", "alt": "Different colored images of the Moon are arranged in a spiral pattern.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-1-colors-of-the-moon/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MoonColors_Pace_960.jpg?w=960&h=991&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 1 - Colors of the Moon</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-1-colors-of-the-moon/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: What color is the Moon? It depends on the night -- and the time. Outside of the Earth&#039;s atmosphere, the dark Moon, which shines by reflected sunlight, appears a magnificently brown-tinged gray. The featured image highlights a collection of apparent colors of the full moon on Earth, documented by one astrophotographer over 10 years from different locations across Italy. A red- or yellow-colored moon usually indicates a moon seen near the horizon. There, some of the blue light has been scattered away by a long path through the Earth&#039;s atmosphere, sometimes laden with fine dust. A blue-colored moon is more rare and can indicate an atmosphere carrying larger dust particles. What created the purple moon is unclear -- it may be a combination of several effects. The last image captures the total lunar eclipse of 2018 July -- where the moon, in Earth&#039;s shadow, appeared a faint red -- due to light refracted through air around the Earth. Have you ever seen an unusually colorful Moon?Tomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"colors of the moon\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Colors of the Moon\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-1-colors-of-the-moon/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MoonColors_Pace_960.jpg?w=960&#038;h=991&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MoonColors_Pace_960.jpg?w=960&#038;h=991&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Different colored images of the Moon are arranged in a spiral pattern.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-01T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-31T10:41:44-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Colors of the Moon\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MoonColors_Pace_960.jpg?w=960&#038;h=991&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 1<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MoonColors_Pace_960.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MoonColors_Pace_960.jpg\" alt=\"Different colored images of the Moon are arranged in a spiral pattern.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Colors of the Moon </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://greenflash.photo/about-me/\">Marcella Giulia Pace</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> What color is the Moon? It depends on the night -- and the time. Outside of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-atmosphere/earths-atmosphere-a-multi-layered-cake/\">Earth's atmosphere</a>, the <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/moon-albedo\">dark Moon</a>, which shines by reflected sunlight, appears a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2003-november-9-apollo-17-lunarscape-a-magnificent-desolation/\">magnificent</a>ly brown-tinged gray. The <a href=\"https://greenflash.photo/portfolio/colors-of-the-moon/\">featured image</a> highlights a collection of apparent colors of the full moon on Earth, documented by one astrophotographer over 10 years from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/G0EUqGhYZ6Y\">different locations</a> across <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a>. A <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-november-11-blood-moon-ice-giant/\">red</a>- or <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">yellow</a>-colored moon usually indicates a moon seen <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-1-buck-moon-and-belt-of-venus/\">near the horizon</a>. There, some of the blue light has been scattered away by a long path through the <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-is-earths-atmosphere/\">Earth's atmosphere</a>, sometimes laden with fine dust. A <a href=\"https://www.loc.gov/everyday-mysteries/categories/astronomy/item/what-is-a-blue-moon-is-it-ever-really-blue/\">blue-colored moon</a> is more rare and can indicate an atmosphere carrying larger <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/a-dusty-journey-146122/\">dust particles</a>. What created the purple moon is <a href=\"https://cdn.akc.org/content/hero/bulldog_rib_bone_hero.jpg\">unclear</a> -- it may be a combination of several effects. The last image captures the total <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-7-two-eclipses-of-saros-133/\">lunar eclipse</a> of 2018 July -- where the moon, in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-25-earths-shadow-visualized-with-lunar-eclipses/\">Earth's shadow</a>, appeared a faint red -- due to light <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Refraction\">refracted</a> through air around the Earth. <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261101\">Have you</a> ever seen an unusually colorful Moon?<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong><a href=\"https://apod.com/fap/ap261102.html\">open space</a>\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-31-night-on-a-spooky-planet/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261101/html"}